olumns out of the source tables and  into a destination table in that  destination table we also join in a  couple additional columns just so all of  the data for this particular report is  available in one table ready to go  having all your data in one table like  that is actually a huge speed-up just in  and of itself as part of this we also  help this team clean up their queries by  making small tweaks here and there such  as removing unnecessary order by clauses  all these things together resulted in a  33% faster average query time and a 45  percent faster end-to-end report  generation time for this use case the  next use case that I'll talk about today  is the AV experimentation project this  team was are already has an analytics  use case running on premise in Vertica  so in this case we're migrating from an  on-premise Vertica cluster into bigquery  the goal of this each case is to fetch  the results of AV experiments on the  twitter product and render those in a UI  users of the UI will actually use the  insights they gained from the UI to  drive product  the interesting thing here is that the  table that powers this UI has the high  hundreds of terabytes of data in one  place we also have a very low latency  requirement because this is powering an  internal dashboard so here we partition  these input tables by time again and  gave it more query slots to execute  again we do this for almost every  project we also clustered these tables  by more than one IV like Jugend  mentioned you can cluster by more than  one field and we want to do this in  order of highest cardinality first to  eliminate the most amount of data in a  query as possible  we also selected only the necessary  columns out of these source tables  whereas the vertical query before was  selecting all of the columns and finally  one of the more interesting performance  optimizations we made was using an array  aggregation clause in bigquery where in  vertical weird had been using a row  number over Clause we use this to  identify the most recent row in a set of  rows to populate part of this UI all of  these things together ended up speeding  up the queries by 55% and the final  project that I'll talk about today is a  tweet analytics project for this project  we want to generate a report of recent  actions on entities the interesting  thing about this one is that we're  migrating from an on-premise solar  cluster into bigquery and also the  bigquery table is populated via bigquery  streaming the table is also massive it's  multiple petabytes of data in a single  table here too we partition the input  tables by time and we gave the project  many many more query slots to work with  we also schedule the projection of the  necessary columns to generate this  report out of the source tables into a  destination table again here too we also  saw a nice speed up with that the most  interesting thing that we saw here  though is we had to tune our storage set  sizes  QWERTY tables are backed by storage sets  which contain files which contain  records storage sets have a maximum size  limit so when a storage set fills up  with records it actually gets committed  and creates a metadata entry with it  associated with the table when a query  wants to run on a table it has to  process all the metadata for the  different storage sets to decide which  stored sets actually need to be accessed  if there's a lot of storage sets there's  a lot of metadata entries and processing  a lot of metadata entries can actually  slow down your query so in our use case  we worked with Google engineering to  increase the size of our storage sets  more storage sets are larger size  storage sense means fewer storage sets  overall because each one can hold more  records because there are fewer storage  sets there is less metadata entries for  a query to have to process which  actually ends up speeding up your query  a good amount when you have a high  volume with streaming inserts into a  table all of these optimizations  together resulted in 33% faster queries  for this ease case and that's the last  week's case I'm going to talk about  today I want to give a huge shout out to  everyone at Twitter cross-functionally  at Google PSO and in Google engineering  for all the help that they have given up  to today Google has helped us build  additional tools for Twitter internal  data format they've helped us build  dashboards and administration tools  they've helped us increase DML  efficiency by changing things in the  back end and they've increased limits in  GCP all over the place for us some of  the most notable ones are for VP CSC or  for the amount of data that a particular  query can process and a single go  without all of these things together we  wouldn't be near where we are today and  we're really looking forward to  continuing our adoption and growth with  bigquery at Twitter so thank you to  everyone who's been involved I'll pass  it back over to John now so he can wrap  up the rest of this presentation I hope  you found this session useful in the  some of the performance tips and  techniques as we've shared with you  today I want to thank Mike  presenter Gary who shared his best  practices of using bigquery at Twitter  and I want to thank you for watching  this session  you
 

